==English==

===Etymology===
{{etyl|la}} {{term|invocare||to call upon}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|US}} {{enPR|in'vōk}}, {{IPA|/ɪnˈvoʊk/}}, {{SAMPA|/In"voUk/}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|invok|ing}}

# {{transitive}} To [[call]] upon (a person, especially a [[god]]) for [[help]], [[assistance]] or [[guidance]].
#* {{quote-book|passage=After marriage, the man had anciently (but this was anterior to Christianity) the power of life and death over his wife. She could '''invoke''' no law against him; he was her sole tribunal and law.|title=[[S:The Subjection of Women|The Subjection of Women]]|author=John Stuart Mill|year=1869}}
#* {{quote-book|passage=The acquisition of a useless part can hardly be said to raise an organism in the natural scale; and in the case of the imperfect, closed flowers, above described, if any new principle has to be '''invoked''', it must be one of retrogression rather than of progression; and so it must be with many parasitic and degraded animals.|year=1872|author=Charles Darwin|title=[[S:The Origin of Species/Chapter VII|The Origin of Species]]}}
#* {{quote-book|passage=It is easier to '''invoke''' or to deplore democracy than to say exactly what it is.|year=1912|author=William Sharp McKechnie|title=[[S:The New Democracy and the Constitution|The New Democracy and the Constitution]]}}
# {{transitive}} To [[conjure]] up with [[incantation]]s.
# {{transitive}} {{computing}} To cause (a [[program]] or [[subroutine]]) to execute.

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|computing|To cause a program or subroutine to execute}} [[call]], [[execute]], [[run]]

